s that got you  interested directly in history did you  do it yourself  uh i did it myself again i think typical  of my generation i didn't i didn't  follow directly the footsteps of my  father i  chose a somewhat different path are you  surprised you ended up in washington dc  yeah that does surprise me and not that  i became an historian but that i'm here  why does it surprise you  it's a long ways away from california i  think many people out here don't realize  that  californians tend to stick to themselves  and they regard washington d.c as  three-quarters of the way to europe  is it a place you'll end up you think or  you go back to california well i've  lived on the east  coast here now for  nearly 20 years pretty soon it'll be  longer than i ever lived in california  so i think i've  made it my home  mr strauss can you tell us where it all  started with you  well i felt my first real brush with  history back when i was a high school  junior i was a page at the united states  supreme court  and that was the year that john kennedy  was assassinated  i remember sitting in the  judge's chambers i was with  justice brennan and justice goldberg and  justice white when the news came  and then within the next couple of days  i  i  watched on pennsylvania avenue as the  funeral  procession passed by and it caused me to  reflect a little bit on the fact that  this was the shining  night of my own parents generation and  through the  the rest of the 1960s while i was at  harvard the same  class that exploded in  riot and strikes although i didn't  participate in that i saw it and it  saddened me it  i was well aware of what my peers were  doing to attack the institutions of  their elders  then as i wrote  the book about how the vietnam war  affected my generation i i saw  really quite a tragic relationship  developing between what we call the gi  generation and the boomers and right  after that of course  the gi's separated into  into  their own culture basically abandoning  the culture for the boomers in return  for a substantial uh  reward in the form of entitlements  programs in return and it wasn't a very  happy been a happy ending to the  generation gap that we all remember  i think that that's how  i i came both to the question of  generations and to a way of looking at  american history a little bit  differently starting in families and and  looking at people moving through time  how did you get to the supreme court  page job in the first place well that  was a result of  the hard work of my gi  parents especially my mother who was a  very typical of the leave it to beaver  kind of  household that we saw in the 1950s a  mother who couldn't do enough for her  children  it was also typical of the kindnesses  that  that people showed towards children back  in those days i i certainly felt as i  was growing up that that the schooling  that people my age received was the most  important task that our community had  and i dare say in the decades since then  children haven't had that same attitude  what did your parents do for a living  well my father received his greatest  pleasure and triumph in world war ii  like many people in his generation and  my mother  like many women of her generation only  was able to discover relatively late in  life her abilities in the public world  and since then she's  become a  a masterful expert in public relations  out in san francisco and she's still  working hard  all right how did you both do that how  did you do this book  how did you physically  write 500 pages and split up the work  um well it was a it was a  a problem with logistics also a problem  with the calendar  bill and i often joke about how we're on  completely different sleep wake cycles  since he's a performer in the evening  and since i get up very early in the  morning our days overlap for about four  hours  but we worked with faxes constant car  transportation  on the phone daily  which is  quite quite something when you when you  have a project that goes on over a  number of years we were breaking new  ground in  a lot of areas that we have about  60 pages of very small type footnotes in  the back and we've we felt that  that we wanted to to give other people  a chance to carry on with this kind of  work there are many generations like the  gi generation of senior citizens nobody  has ever written a biography about this  generation before it's remarkable when  you consider their own strong connection  to history there have been dozens of  biographies of boomers  but and a few of the silent but none of  the gi's and none of the 13th generation  and  we had to sort through all of american  history and we found only the very very  isolated example of someone who had  taken a peer group from birth  and followed them all the way through  old age let me go back to the basics how  did you do  how did you do it in other words how did  you split it up well what we had to do  to write this book to write the history  of abraham lincoln's generation for  example you had to find a history of  childhood  and see what was going on around the  1800s and 1810s you had to find a  history of  of  youth or religion or or great  spiritual and abolitionist movements to  find out what young people were doing in  the 1830s you had to look at it at  histories of the family to find out what  people were doing and forming families  around the 1830s and 40s then then you'd  look at it at a traditional history book  to find out what they were doing at the  peak of power and then you would look at  histories of  old age to find out how they were  treated when old we had to to take all  of those pieces and write a completely  separate story to come back to this  whole question of reconstructing history  along what we call the generational  diagonal following the same group  through time you know you can read a lot  of  and some of them very fine books written  about the history of childhood and  they'll describe the street urchins of  the 1890s and then suddenly a couple  chapters later you'll get the boy scouts  of the 1920s  but of course these aren't the same  people these are different people what  we had to do was take the histories of  childhood the histories of adolescence  of marriage of old age and refit the  pieces together so that they're talking  about the same people over time and this  is really at the core of what the whole  purpose of this book is about again  though did you write one chapter and  bill write another we really both worked  on the whole book  we really did some of us would write  first drafts of different chapters like  i guess you could say i wrote more of  the first drafts of the of the 19th and  20th century century generations and  neil of the 17th and 18th generation but  we both worked on all of that by the  time we got to the end i don't think  you'll notice any stylistic gaps as you  read through did you ever disagree on  anything  not very much we worked it out well it  was a kind of a creative  process in which we would come across  something new and and we  always had to test it against our idea  of the theory and our own idea of the  theory of course evolved over time as we  saw what was actually happening out  there now for example in each of the  of the  chapters  uh that we have we divide the book into  really three parts one part to explain  what the generation is why it's  important and how it connects with  people in their daily lives  uh a second section in which we we and  it's the longest section in which we  tell the entire story of america as a  biography of 18 generations and we we  divide each of these  generations up into phases of life we  have a little section on their youth  coming of age rising adult midlife and  we deal with history always from the  prism of this life cycle  framework and it's important to say we  spend as much time on their childhood as  we spend on how they behaved as  presidents  bill strauss you said that you started  seven years ago and neil you joined him  five years ago when did you actually  start writing this book and when did you  finish it  well we  had to spend years doing research for it  collecting clippings about the modern  generations and  doing a tremendous amount of reading we  probably had  our houses are still littered with  hundreds of photocopies of articles uh  copies of books binders filled with  material because again piecing this  together along this diagonal was was  arduous work it required going through  many books just to get uh work out one  point of history did you computerize all  this of course  this book could not have been written  longhand i assure you  is there something the computer made  available today to be able to do this  you wouldn't have been able to do this  30 years ago well there's one very  important thing that the computer made  available and that's what we have in the  appendix and this is something that's uh  that's very new data  uh it's part of our second appendix  where we show the the  generation  the generational makeup of the national  leadership  uh by  year  and there are some startling things that  you discover in that for example if you  go to the next page over here you'll  find a  you'll find the largest generational  landslide in american history happened  in the years immediately following the  civil war when the the transcendental  generation which held the largest  generational plurality ever seen 90 of  all the  members of congress and senators and  governors were transcendental at the  outbreak of the civil war in  1869  there was a 19-point swing from abraham  lincoln's generation to ulysses grants  the biggest ever seen and it showed the  exhaustion that american society faced  basically voters were throwing out the  older reformers who had brought such  tragedy to the country and putting in  the younger pragmatists then when we  look at more modern generations we can  see  in  in our own time that the great 89th  congress what sometimes is called the  grandfather of entitlements programs was  right when the gi's were at the peak of  their power 75 percent approximately of  the members of congress and senate and  state houses were  were gis and they used that power  enormously to enact the great society  agenda the silent emerged into power  right around the time of  watergate and  the the ford and carter years right  around the time the country was  beginning to feel that it was spinning  its wheels and unable to solve problems  when you look at the boom generation you  can see that we're still at only 21  percent but we're at approximately the  same phase of life as  franklin roosevelt's missionary  generation was around 1910 and over the  decade that followed they swept into  office and with it they brought a a very  stern new morals regime to the country  and that's one of the things that we  predict that will happen in the 1990s  let me ask you uh  go back to you we talked about senator  gore earlier yeah he saw the book he  read it he gave you a tremendous uh  boost here on the on the flap let's say  he's thinking about running for  president and he picks up your book and  looks at it what does it tell him he's a  boomer he's a he is a boomer yes what  does it tell him about his possibility  of being elected president well  so far the silent generation has not  done a very good job of electing  presidents and we may very well go from  a gi to a boom whether it's democratic  or republican i don't know i guess i'm  in the silent generation if you're  between 48 and 65 you're in the silent  generation born between four no that's  no if you are age age i am i'm 49 yeah  and you you uh you were  you came of age just too late to  participate  as a hero in world war ii and just too  early to feel the heat of the vietnam  draft again go back to senator gore  because there i assume there are a  number of boomers that are out there we  don't have to use him necessarily by  name but senator kerry  i think  i i think whether it's going to tell  people of the boom generation who are in  politics or interested in entering  politics incidentally one of our  predictions is in the 1990s we're going  to see a lot of boomers coming into  politics from other professions other  walks of life the long-time incumbent  politician that is very characteristic  of the silent generation may become a  fading artifact and may we may begin to  have a swifter turnover in in  congressional seats  but what it's going to say is that among  his peers who are going to begin to  dominate the electorate in terms of  voting clout  are going to take a uh a sterner more  values oriented approach to the problems  of the country  this is everyone is already talking  about the 1990s as the decade of  austerity the decade of savings the  decade we have to sort of pay back for  some of the sins we've made in the 1970s  and 80s and a lot of this is boomer  driven we think we think this is  characteristic of this type of  generation moving into midlife  one of the targets uh uh uh  both intentionally and accidentally of  the of the morals campaign you might say  of the boomers will be younger people to  clean up the world or rectify the  mistakes of youth and of course this is  part of what we what we talk about in  creating this new safer more protected  nurture of the young millennial  generation yeah the other days tend to  look upon thirteeners as a as an army of  aging bart symptoms bart simpsons the  brat pack the kids who who uh  are a little too wild not well educated  and don't have that much to offer and  the kids who are now in their twenties  the thirteeners resent this as they try  to find their own way in a world that  they perceive is very difficult for them  in fact uh gore is a senator gore's wife  tipper gore is actively involved in in  the movement to  uh  to  to an extent clean up some of the some  of the lyrics and some of what's going  on in  in the youth culture and this is this is  an important boomers feel very strongly  about that because they felt to an  extent they were burned by that and they  feel the damage it can do to children in  a way that the silent generation who has  been running our culture  since the early 70s never felt that  damage because of course they had an  extremely conformist almost a smothering  childhood and they they have no  first-hand experience at what the the  danger of under protection can do to  children well we think that politicians  or marketers in particular product  salesmen who are concerned about how to  reach generations should think about as  they read our book  and try to decide how to either get  elected or launch a new product line is  look real hard at the section of our  book that will certainly be the most  controversial with historians  and that is  we have a whole section on the future a  50 page chapter on what we say the cycle  tells us about what the future will be  and in this chapter  is chapter 13 and  we call it completing the millennial  cycle and and what we do is not try to  predict specific events  but rather how the national mood will  shift and how each of today's  generations will behave as as they grow  a little bit older  and in the past what what people have  done is uh tended to assume that uh that  there won't be any any change in the way  older people will behave or that that 25  year olds will still be like 25 year  olds used to be and that has has not  happened in the past couple of decades  it's never happened in american history  today for example uh  if you were to  reflect on each phase of life  it's completely different from what it  was back in the nineteen sixties our  elders are busy and active and happy our  uh uh people in midlife are sensitive  compassionate caring concerned about  cultural pluralism the people in their  thirties the boomers are are  self-immersed and uh  and uninclined to follow the directions  set by other people very much unlike the  silent and in in the next 20 years or so  you're going to see changes just as  dramatic what's your biggest surprise as  you go around and do the shows what  questions are you asked that  or that you  don't expect or what are the ones you're  asked the most often  well one one thing that we've noticed as  we've talked to people is is the  different response that people give us  based upon what their generation is  we find for example that gi's today's  seniors are very history absorbed and  they they are quite concerned about  their place in history and so they're  interested in in this they also notice  that no one has ever written the  biography of their generation before and  they're interested in what we have to  say again gis are what age uh they're 66  on up born between 1901 and 1924 just  just to add to that  the way we tell the story of the gi  generation is we we explain what they  became as adults and in the 1950s and  60s by looking at how elders saw them as  children how special and good they saw  and deserving they saw them as children  and to a lot of gis today they  understand that that's a story that  makes sense to them and we also try to  show the gis and something that they  they really are drawn to that today's  little millennial children so far look  like a generation much like their own  and  most importantly that the boomer yuppies  that so many seniors today find  distasteful those cultural  perfectionists we all know about  are  very very similar in personality to the  gi's own parents the missionary  generation of franklin roosevelt the  problem was you see the gis never knew  them when they were young adults okay  i'm in the silent generation so what  kind of questions have i asked you that  would fit my generation well the silent  generation come much harder to the  notion that there is such a thing as a  generation because silent people tend  not to perceive themselves as much as  being part of a bona fide generation  they look at the powerful gis just older  and the  self-absorbed  boomers younger and they're more  inclined to say well everybody's people  and we're all diverse and we have to be  concerned about all these different  cultures and fragments of history we  also find just in general among the  silent generation a strong impulse  toward  towards making things complex they like  to see they don't like labels they don't  like generalizations this is part of  their whole life mission to take a  culture that was so simple and to break  it up to show how people are different  to show how people each have their own  tendencies their own desires and  basically to bring out the variety in  humanity this is what's made the silence  so effective in dealing with people i've  got to ask bill strauss as the leader of  the capitol steps when you go into  entertain a group  based on your  this book and your knowledge of history  can you tell in advance depending on  what age the audience is going to be  what they're going to their reaction is  going to be to some degree i can yes i  think all of today's generations have an  appreciation for humor which is a little  bit different i i will say one thing the  silent generation has produced the  greatest  ever american comedians and if you if  you trace  uh from the little rascals through  shirley temple to  jerry lewis stan freeburg  tom lehrer all the way up to  mark russell and many of the other  comedians of today they're born very  nearly at the same time what's your  favorite audience though when you when  you go out there i know it's hard to  label but  they're they're all good brian  you do have to have a sense of of who  your audience is but as long as you have  that sense and you know what they're  looking for they're all good  neil last question again any other  questions uh that you're surprised about  being asked as you go out and talk about  your book on the circuit  uh i think the what bill was talking  about the the generational division  among our response is uh  homers in particular the boomers  understand what a bona fide generation  is and they also are looking for  simplifying visions  very much unlike the silent  and they know that they're moving  somewhere beyond hippie and yuppie they  also are intrigued by the idea that they  are not the first generation of hippies  and yuppies you can look back at abraham  lincoln's transcendentals or franklin  roosevelt's missionaries and you see  much the same life path and they're  intrigued by the notion that when  they're old they'll become what we call  grey champions  time's up william strauss  neil howe co-authors of this book the  history of america's future  1584-2069 generations the name of the  book thank you both for joining us  thank you thank you brian  [Music]  join us next week on booknotes when we  discuss the riddle of power presidential  leadership from truman to bush a new  book by robert shogun national political  correspondent for the los angeles times  drawing on his 30 years of experience  covering campaigns mr shogun examines  the use of authority by the most recent  nine u.s presidents and discusses how  their use of power results in popular  success or political gridlock  if you would like a transcript of this  week's program send five dollars to tape  writer incorporated box 885 lincolnshire  illinois  zero six nine  [Music]  yes  [Music]  you
 

